threadpool: NULL-guard get_task_wsi before task_status in HTTP_WRITEABLE

From: Andy Green <andy@warmcat.com>

LWS_CALLBACK_HTTP_WRITEABLE did:

  n = lws_threadpool_task_status(lws_threadpool_get_task_wsi(wsi), &_user);

lws_threadpool_get_task_wsi() returns NULL when wsi->tp_task_owner.head
is NULL (lib/misc/threadpool/threadpool.c), which happens once the bound
task has been reaped or disassociated - for example after
LWS_CALLBACK_HTTP_DROP_PROTOCOL dequeued it, or after core close ran
lws_threadpool_wsi_closing().  lws_threadpool_task_status() then
dereferences the NULL task pointer (task->tp) and faults.

This is racy under load: a late WRITEABLE for a wsi whose task was
already cleaned up crashes the process, which looks externally exactly
like the "lockup" since the server stops responding.

Fetch the task into a local first and return 0 when there is none, the
same pattern the canonical minimal-ws-server-threadpool example uses.
---
 src/protocol_gitohashi.c |   16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/protocol_gitohashi.c b/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
index 622a623..dc9e24a 100644
--- a/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
+++ b/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
@@ -350,6 +350,7 @@ callback_gitohashi(struct lws *wsi, enum lws_callback_reasons reason,
 	struct pss_gitohashi *pss = (struct pss_gitohashi *)user;
 	struct lws_threadpool_create_args cargs;
 	struct lws_threadpool_task_args targs;
+	struct lws_threadpool_task *task;
 	struct task_data_gitohashi *priv;
 	struct jg2_vhost_config config;
 	const char *csize, *flags, *z;
@@ -653,7 +654,20 @@ callback_gitohashi(struct lws *wsi, enum lws_callback_reasons reason,
 		if (!pss)
 			break;
 
-		n = lws_threadpool_task_status(lws_threadpool_get_task_wsi(wsi), &_user);
+		/*
+		 * lws_threadpool_get_task_wsi() returns NULL once the task
+		 * bound to this wsi has been reaped/disassociated (e.g. it
+		 * was dequeued by HTTP_DROP_PROTOCOL, or core close ran
+		 * lws_threadpool_wsi_closing()).  lws_threadpool_task_status()
+		 * dereferences the task pointer, so we must not pass it NULL.
+		 * The canonical minimal threadpool example guards this the
+		 * same way.
+		 */
+		task = lws_threadpool_get_task_wsi(wsi);
+		if (!task)
+			return 0;
+
+		n = lws_threadpool_task_status(task, &_user);
 		lwsl_info("%s: LWS_CALLBACK_SERVER_WRITEABLE: %p: "
 			   "priv %p, status %d\n", __func__, wsi, _user, n);
 		switch(n) {
